Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Map-Maker's Solstice
- Story & Context: On the midnight express crossing the disputed border at San Pedro, a lone cartographer began tapping out a 124 BPM pulse on a pocket synth to drown out the noise of border guards searching the outer cars. Mateo overheard the high-register synth stabs, pulled out his pocket-trumpet, and laid a soaring, tropical melody over the low rumble of the engine while fuzz bass rattled the metal seats. The track captures that sudden, euphoric pivot from terror to communal dance, immortalized when the train finally burst into the dawn light at the coast. "We drew the line right through the water," yelled a street vendor who was there, giving the track its iconic vocal sample.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A sun-drenched, high-contrast visual of an old nautical map laid over a rusted iron train table, illuminated by a beam of harsh golden sunlight. Overlaid with bold neon-yellow typography displaying the lyric '"We drew the line right through the water"', while ocean waves mist across the metal train tracks visible through a dirty window frame, cinematic medium shot.

Track 2: Desolation Bay (Sub-Fuzz Horn)
- Story & Context: Written during a three-day heatwave inside a coastal depot, this track pays tribute to the forgotten port of Desolation Bay, where abandoned shipping containers become makeshift sound-systems every Saturday night. Local trumpet legend Dona Beatrice brought her horn down to the docks, blowing mournful, minor-key solos over a staggering 125 BPM progressive house beat that felt like a slow-moving tectonic plate. A commuter captured her crying into the mic after a 12-minute jam session, her voice echoing over the heavy bass sub-tones. "Leave my bones where the tide runs hot," she whispered into the delay pedal, creating the most famous hook in the entire regional scene.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Artistic album art showing a rusted steel shipping container sitting on a blinding white tropical beach under an intense noon sun. Vibrant pink tropical flowers grow over heavy amplifiers stacked in the sand, with bold translucent glass lettering reading '"Leave my bones where the tide runs hot"', stylized 80s film grain, ultra-detailed texture.

Track 3: What Did You See at the Boundary Stone?
- Story & Context: The final track on the EP became an anthem among night-shift commuters after a legendary dawn performance on the open-air platform of the terminus station. The track opens with clean, glassy pocket-synth arpeggios that build for four agonizing minutes before dropping into a wall of Drop-F doom fuzz and soaring, brass-driven progression. The title comes from a graffiti tag scrawled across the final kilometre marker of the coastal railway line, where the rails literally sink into the sea. "Ask the sun when it hits the iron," chanted the crowd of insomniacs as the sun rose, providing the track with its haunting, triumphant final chorus.
